json DailySpinSpace {
    doc """
    One space on the Daily Spin wheel.

    Stored in the Global wiki's column order
    (https://bravefrontierglobal.fandom.com/wiki/Daily_Spin), which the wiki
    labels red/blue/green/yellow/gray/purple.

    ⚠ THOSE COLOUR NAMES ARE THE WIKI'S, NOT THE CLIENT'S WEDGE POSITIONS.
    setupSpinWheelRewards @0xE52B40 assigns each row an angle by its index
    within the group, from a 5-entry table at 0x230FDE4 - 60/120/180/240/300
    degrees - and index 0 skips the load entirely, keeping the pre-zeroed 0
    degrees.  So group index 0 is drawn at the POINTER (the top, which the
    artwork colours blue), not at the wiki's red column.  What matters is that
    this array is in the same ORDER as the client's group rows; the colours are
    only a naming convention carried over from the source table.

    This table exists ONLY so the server can award the prize.  What the player
    SEES is drawn from the client's own F_SG_DAILYLOGIN_REWARDS_MST.

    ⚠ IT IS NO LONGER TRANSCRIBED FROM THE WIKI, AND MUST NOT BE AGAIN.  The
    client caches that very table under LocalState as Ver14_ni16xAsw.dat, and
    tools/gen_daily_spin_archive.py decrypts it and regenerates this file from
    it - so the two tables are now the SAME data rather than two readings of
    one page.  The wiki version was wrong in three ways at once, each of which
    reached a player: the ids are 1-based not 0-based, there are 32 groups not
    29, and every space carries a rate the uniform draw was ignoring.
    """

    field kind type=str {
        key "kind"
        doc """
        How to award this space: gem, karma, zel, ticket, item or unit.

        These route onto the primitives the server already has rather than
        introducing another reward switch (handbook 7.13): gem/karma/zel/ticket
        are userinfo columns, item is gme::addUserItem and unit is
        gme::addUserUnit.
        """
    }

    field name type=str {
        key "name"
        doc "Human-readable prize name, straight from the wiki table.  Used in logs so a mis-set space is legible without resolving ids."
    }

    field id type="u32::int" {
        key "id"
        doc "item_mst or unit_mst id for kind=item / kind=unit.  Zero and ignored for the currency kinds, which carry their amount in count."
    }

    field count type="u32::int" {
        key "count"
        doc "Quantity awarded."
    }

    field rate_x100 type="u32::int" {
        key "rate_x100"
        doc """
        Chance of this space in HUNDREDTHS OF A PERCENT, so 32.00% is 3200 and
        a group's six sum to 10000.  From `ug9xV4Fz` in the client's table.

        Stored scaled rather than as a float on purpose.  The client gives the
        rate to exactly two decimal places, so hundredths are exact and the
        weighted draw is integer arithmetic end to end - no rounding to argue
        about, and no dependence on which floating-point type the generator's
        `pkg::float64` resolves to (it degrades to `long double` on MSVC, which
        glaze refuses to parse).

        ⚠ THE DRAW IS WEIGHTED, NOT UNIFORM.  The server picked a space with a
        flat RandomUInt(0, 5) until 2026-09-20, which handed out the 1-percent
        Gem as often as the 32-percent Karma - a sixfold inflation of the
        rarest prize on the wheel, on the one reward that gates summoning.
        """
    }

    field available type="bool::bool" {
        key "available"
        doc """
        False when the prize has no id in the MST tables we hold.

        Now always true: reading the table out of the client resolved the nine
        rewards the wiki transcription could not place (the Mecha Gods, Burst
        Emperor, Almighty Imp Arton and Crescent Dew), because the client names
        them by id rather than by English name.  The field stays because the
        grant path must still refuse to award id 0 if a future table drifts.
        """
    }
}

json DailySpinDay {
    doc """
    One group of the Daily Spin table, which for groups 1-31 is one cycle day.

    The client's table holds 32 groups.  Days 1-28 climb; groups 29, 30 and 31
    are byte-identical and are the wiki's "Day 29+" resting row; group 32 is six
    Gems and is the GUARANTEED-GEM wheel, not a 32nd day - see GEM_GROUP in
    tools/gen_daily_spin_archive.py for the evidence and for what would
    disprove it.
    """

    field day type="u32::int" {
        key "day"
        doc "Group number, 1-based.  For 1-31 this is also the cycle day."
    }

    field guaranteed_gem_on_first_spin type="bool::bool" {
        key "guaranteed_gem_on_first_spin"
        doc """
        True on days 7, 14, 21 and 28, where the live game guaranteed 1 Gem on
        the first spin of the day regardless of where the wheel stopped.

        A rule layered on top of the six spaces, not a seventh space - and on
        those days the server points the client at group 32, whose every space
        is a Gem, so the wheel it draws agrees with the prize it pays.
        """
    }

    field spaces type="[DailySpinSpace]" {
        key "spaces"
        doc """
        The six spaces, in the client's group-row order - which is the wiki's
        column order, and NOT its colour layout.  Index 0 is whatever the wheel
        draws under the pointer.  See DailySpinSpace for why the two differ.
        """
    }
}
